{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-atom-knot-unique","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Proposition","doco:Section"],"label":"prop:atom-knot-unique","kind":"proposition","title":"Uniqueness of the atom-knot up to Markov equivalence","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-atom-knot-exists"],"tags":["uniqueness","atom-knot","markov-equivalence"],"leanRef":"qou:QOU.BraidKnot.AtomKnot.unique_up_to_markov","text":"atom-knot-unique.md","leanSource":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/knots-particles-confinement/atom-knot-unique.lean","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"prop:atom-knot-unique\"></a><p><em>The atom-knot of type <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(Z, N)</code> is unique up to Markov equivalence of braid representatives.</em></p>\n<p><em>Suppose <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\beta_1</code> and <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\beta_2</code> are two braids in <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">B_{n(K)}</code> that both realize the normal form block decomposition <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_w = T_{w_{q^Z}} \\cdot T_{w_{\\bar q^N}} \\cdot T_{w_{\\mathrm{inter}}}</code>. Then their Markov closures are isotopic in <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">S^3</code>:</em></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-math math-display\">  \\hat\\beta_1 \\;\\cong_{\\mathrm{isotopy}}\\; \\hat\\beta_2.</code></pre>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/knots-particles-confinement/atom-knot-unique.md","heading":"Proposition","number":"9.10.3"}
